<p>The Justice Department is preparing to seek criminal charges against former Cuban President Raúl Castro, 94, and federal prosecutors are expected to unseal an indictment in Miami on May 20 if a grand jury approves it, a U.S. Justice Department official and people familiar with the matter said. The expected case focuses on the 1996 Cuban shootdown of two aircraft operated by Brothers to the Rescue, a Miami-based exile group, that killed four people; Castro was Cuba’s defense minister at the time. The planned indictment follows President Trump’s threats of possible military action and an economic blockade that cut fuel shipments, contributing to blackouts, food insecurity and wider economic strain on the island. Cuba’s government did not respond to AP and NBC News requests for comment on the possible indictment, while Cuban Foreign Minister Bruno Rodríguez said at a BRICS foreign ministers’ meeting that Cuba would continue on its “path of sovereignty” despite the U.S. embargo, sanctions and threats of force.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dyfy_IJT9EMzkbyhm7SNw_Pi4oJkz4128X_eA56pJRGVz0PBFr6WkxzEXHK5JGjEMYUDoRImVWCy8H5zY5V4Du-hpdszBOL_ghDiUzBUfZGEiNAI9AXRk-QfaqMSNYXyYqo-8f5BMPEuw3qebWJsoo9eGuZpY8XblodcbrqYo0QEplqYi0OKAK-2qe1ZBvC1ViavXprKP0B9nHyKmht6FGJsG0VpCc9klCSW-N-HP9vsCDMPRdyS7j4bs5t8ISWtc2MeYzrorfKIexiOUw' alt='Cuba’s former president Raúl Castro attending a May Day parade in Havana.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Planned venue: Spanish-language accounts citing the Miami Herald said the announcement would be tied to a Freedom Tower event marking Cuba’s Independence Day and honoring those killed in the shootdown.</li><li>Enforcement issue: Cuban military officers previously charged in the shootdown case remained outside U.S. custody because they were never extradited.</li><li>CIA contacts: Cuban officials said Ratcliffe’s Havana visit included a meeting with the interior minister, while Washington has continued talks with Havana and pressure on Cuba.</li><li>Florida reaction: Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis responded favorably to the CBS report on X, writing: “Adelante, ya era hora”.</li></ul><blockquote>I’ve been wishing for justice to be served, justice to be realized. - José Basulto</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: Reuters-based accounts said the administration has called Cuba’s current government corrupt and incompetent and is seeking to replace it, while talks with Havana have continued. (<a href='https://journalstar.com/news/nation-world/crime-courts/article_32c45ca6-5ea8-598b-887f-b91cce1669a1.html'>Journal Star</a>)</li><li>Cubans in Havana: Reuters interviews cited by Aaj found concern that prosecuting Castro would roll back negotiations; Sonia Torres, a schoolteacher, called it an affront to Cuban pride. (<a href='https://english.aaj.tv/news/330459049/us-considers-indicting-raul-castro-over-1996-plane-shootdown'>Aaj English TV</a>)</li><li>Brothers to the Rescue families: Relatives of those killed said anyone involved in the shootdown, not only Castro, should be held accountable. (<a href='https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/brothers-to-the-rescue-founder-wishing-for-justice-amid-possible-raul-castro-indictment/3809790/'>NBC Miami</a>)</li><li>Richard Feinberg: The UC San Diego professor emeritus said an indictment could appeal to South Florida voters but would not necessarily persuade the U.S. military to pursue a second war of choice near Florida. (<a href='https://ksltv.com/world-news/trump-raul-castro-indictment-cuba/909271/'>KSLTV</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/trump-administration-prepares-to-seek-raul-castro-indictment-as-it-pressures-cuba-ap-sources-say'>Trump administration prepares to seek Raúl Castro indictment as it pressures Cuba, AP sources say</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/who-is-raul-castro-the-cuban-leader-facing-a-us-indictment'>Who is Raul Castro, the Cuban leader facing